<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Sudan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> is a landlocked country in east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> with its capital at Juba. It became independent from Sudan on 9 July 2011 after a referendum held under the peace agreement of 2005 that ended Sudan's second civil war. Africa's newest state fell into civil war in December 2013, and fighting between forces loyal to Salva Kiir and Riek Machar displaced millions of people before a peace agreement in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-sudan.jpg" alt="South Sudan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achim1999 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Sudan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> lies between latitudes 3° and 13°N and is covered in tropical forest, swamps and grassland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> river system runs south to north across its centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, known locally as the Bahr al Jabal, passes Juba and forms the Sudd, a large swamp of seasonally flooded grassland. Habitats include plateaus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannahs</a>, floodplains and wetlands, and Bandingilo National Park hosts one of the largest wildlife migrations in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nilotic peoples of the region, among them the Dinka, Anyuak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a>, Nuer and Shilluk, first entered South Sudan sometime before the tenth century. From the 15th to the 19th century migrations, largely from the area of Bahr el Ghazal, brought the Anyuak, Dinka, Nuer and Shilluk to their modern locations, while the Acholi and Bari settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a>. The Zande and others, who entered in the 16th century, established the region's largest state, in Equatoria. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avungara">Avungara</a> sib rose to power over the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a> society.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Khedive Ismail Pasha first tried to control the region in the 1870s, setting up the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a>; its governors were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> from 1869, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> from 1874 and Emin Pasha from 1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> was governed as an Anglo-Egyptian condominium from 1899 until independence in 1956. British policies favouring Christian missionaries, such as the Closed District Ordinance of 1922, together with the swamplands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, limited the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to the south. Colonial policy emphasised the development of the Arabised north and largely ignored the south, which lacked schools, hospitals, roads and bridges.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>'s first independent elections in 1958, continued neglect of the south by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> government led to revolts and the longest civil war on the continent. Following the First Sudanese Civil War the Southern Sudan Autonomous Region was formed in 1972 and lasted until 1983, when the Second Sudanese Civil War began. It ended with the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of 2005, and southern autonomy was restored. More than 2 million people are said to have died in the wars and more than 4 million were displaced. South Sudan became independent on 9 July 2011 after 98.8% voted for independence in a referendum in January 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and its capital and largest city is Juba. Its transitional constitution, in force since 9 July 2011, sets up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a> with a two-house National Legislature. The population is composed mostly of Nilotic peoples, the Dinka being the largest group, followed by the Nuer and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a>. The common languages are Juba Arabic and English. A civil war was fought from 2013 to 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Sudan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ugandan forces have since been involved in the wars of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, the conflicts of <b>South Sudan</b> and the African Union mission in Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>South Sudan</b> the paramount chief of each payam works alongside an administrator appointed by the government.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>South Sudan</b> declared independence from Sudan after a referendum, becoming the world's newest internationally recognised state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The reth survives today as a traditional ruler in <b>South Sudan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/shilluk-kingdom">Shilluk Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
